### 10\. Conditional Statements in JavaScript

Conditional statements are used to execute different blocks of code based on conditions.

There are 4 types of conditional statements:

* if statement

* if-else statement

* else-if ladder

* switch statement

### 1\. if Statement

\- The `if` statement executes a block of code only when the condition is true.

Syntax:

```javascript
if (condition) {
    // statements
}
```

Example:

```javascript
let age = 20;

if (age >= 18) {
    console.log("Eligible to Vote");
}
```

Output:

```text
Eligible to Vote
```

### 2\. if-else Statement

\- The `if-else` statement executes one block when the condition is true and another block when the condition is false.

Syntax:

```javascript
if (condition) {
    // statements
} else {
    // statements
}
```

Example:

```javascript
let age = 16;

if (age >= 18) {
    console.log("Eligible to Vote");
} else {
    console.log("Not Eligible to Vote");
}
```

Output:

```text
Not Eligible to Vote
```

### 3\. else-if Ladder

* The `else-if` ladder is used to check multiple conditions.

* When one condition becomes true, its corresponding block is executed.

Syntax:

```javascript
if (condition1) {
    // statements
} else if (condition2) {
    // statements
} else if (condition3) {
    // statements
} else {
    // statements
}
```

Example:

```javascript
let marks = 85;

if (marks >= 90) {
    console.log("Grade A");
} else if (marks >= 75) {
    console.log("Grade B");
} else if (marks >= 50) {
    console.log("Grade C");
} else {
    console.log("Fail");
}
```

Output:

```text
Grade B
```

### 4\. switch Statement

* The `switch` statement is used to execute one block of code from multiple options.

* It compares the given expression with different case values.

* `break` is used to stop execution from moving to the next case.

* `default` executes when no case matches.

Syntax:

```javascript
switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // statements
        break;

    case value2:
        // statements
        break;

    default:
        // statements
}
```

Example:

```javascript
let day = 2;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        console.log("Monday");
        break;

    case 2:
        console.log("Tuesday");
        break;

    case 3:
        console.log("Wednesday");
        break;

    default:
        console.log("Invalid Day");
}
```

Output:

```text
Tuesday
```

### Comparison Table

| Statement | Usage                                  |
| --------- | -------------------------------------- |
| if        | Checks one condition                   |
| if-else   | Checks true or false                   |
| else-if   | Checks multiple conditions             |
| switch    | Selects one case from multiple options |

